<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s09e06" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 6: Slope-Intercept Form, Part 1</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 6 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Given a line’s equation in slope-intercept form, quickly construct the graph
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Given a line’s slope and y-intercept, construct the equation in slope-intercept form
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Given the graph of a line, construct the equation in slope-intercept form
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 6 Key Terminology</title>
    <p>
      Slope-intercept form of a line
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    An equation whose graph is a straight line can be written in the form:
    <md>
      y = mx  +b
    </md>
    The letters <m>m</m> and <m>b</m> stand for numbers that tell you the shape and position of the line:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          The number <m>m</m> is the line's slope
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The number <m>b</m> comes from the line's y-intercept <m>(0,b)</m>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    This “template” for the equation of a line is called “slope-intercept” form. 
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      A line has the equation <m>y=3x+2</m>. Find the line’s slope and the line’s y-intercept. Then draw the line’s graph. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: The slope “m” is the coefficient of <m>x</m> in the equation. Here, the coefficient of <m>x</m> is 3, so the slope is 3.
    </p>
    <p>
      The “b,” related to the line’s y-intercept, is the number added in the back of the equation. 
      Here, the number added in the back is 2. So, the y-intercept is the point (0,2).
    </p>
    <p>
      Now let’s draw the line. 
      </p>
      <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription>a graph of the intercept (0,2)</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-96ex1a" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 3*x +2       
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <point p="(0,2)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
    <p>
      Then, use the fact that the slope is 3=<m>\frac{1}{3}</m> = rise/run . From the point (0,2), 
      go over 1 unit and then go up 3 units, and mark another point at this new location. 
    </p>
    <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription>a graph of the intercept (0,2) and point (1,5)</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-96ex1b" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 3*x +2       
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        <point p="(0,2)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
          <point p="(1,5)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          Now draw a straight line through the two points you marked:
        </p>
        <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription>a graph of the intercept (0,2) and point (1,5)</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-96ex1c" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 3*x +2       
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
             <point p="(0,2)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
          <point p="(1,5)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
        <graph function = "f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      A line has the equation <m>2y+4x=6</m>. Find the line’s slope and y-intercept. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: The line is not currently written in slope-intercept form. 
      It can be converted to slope-intercept form by solving for y (see Season 07, Episode 10: Rearranging Formulas). 
      <md>
        <mrow> 2y+4x\amp =6</mrow>
        <mrow> 2y+4x-4x\amp =6-4x</mrow>
        <mrow> 2y\amp =6-4x</mrow>
        <mrow> \frac{2y}{2}\amp = \frac{6-4x}{2}</mrow>
        <mrow> y\amp = \frac{6}{2}-\frac{4x}{2}</mrow>
        <mrow> y\amp =3-2x</mrow>
        <mrow> y\amp =-2x+3</mrow>
      </md>
       Now we can see that the line’s slope is <m>m=-2</m> and its y-intercept is (0,3).
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Find the equation of the line graphed below
    </p>
     <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription>a graph of a line sloping down</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-96ex2a" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = -x/2+1      
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
                    <graph function = "f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          Solution: Start by identifying the graph’s y-intercept. This is the point where the graph crosses the vertical axis. 
        </p>
         <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription>a graph of a line sloping down with the y-intercept marked at (0,1)</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-96ex2b" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = -x/2+1      
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
             <point p="(0,1)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
                  <graph function = "f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          The y-intercept is (0,1), which tells you the “b” in the slope-intercept template is <m>b=1</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now determine the slope of the line. Mark two easy-to-read points on the line and measure the rise and the run between them. 
        </p>
         <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription>a graph of a line sloping down</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-96ex2c" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = -x/2+1      
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
             <point p="(0,1)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
          <point p="(2,0)" stroke="blue" fill="blue"/>
            <line endpoints="(0,1),(2,1)" stroke="green"/>
            <line endpoints="(2,1),(2,0)" stroke="green"/>
        <graph function="f"/>
        <label anchor="(1,1)" alignment="north">
        run
        </label>
        <label anchor ="(2,0.5)" alignment="east">
        rise
        </label>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          The run is 2 units and the rise is -1 units. Notice that the rise is negative because the graph goes down as we follow it from left to right. So the slope is
          <md>
            m = \frac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}} = \frac{2}{-1} = -2
          </md>
          Now we put our values of <m>m</m> and <m>b</m> into the slope-intercept form template:
          <md>
            <mrow>y= \amp mx+b </mrow>
            <mrow>y= \amp -2x+1 </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
  </example>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s09e06">
    <title>Season 9, Episode 6 Practice Problems</title>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Write a linear equation in slope-intercept form given the following information
        </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="0.5in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>m = 3</m>, <m>b = -4</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.5in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>m = -\frac{1}{5}</m>, <m>b = 2.75</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.5in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              slope = -1, y-intercept: <m>(0, 4)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.5in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              slope = 3.3, y-intercept: <m>(0, -9.7)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Write the equation of the following graphed lines.
        </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <image width="45%">
              <prefigure label="pf-96pp1" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 4*x-3     
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
             
                  <graph function = "f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
          </statement>
          </task>
          <task>
          <statement>
            <image width="45%">
              <prefigure label="pf-96pp2" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = -x/3+1    
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
           
                  <graph function = "f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <image width="45%">
              <prefigure label="pf-96pp3" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 5 - 3*x   
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
         
                  <graph function = "f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
          </statement>
          </task>
          <task>
          <statement>
            <image width="45%">
              <prefigure label="pf-96pp4" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
        <definition>f(x) = 2*x/5-2    
        </definition>
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
             
                  <graph function = "f"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
          </statement>
          </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          For the followin equations, identify the slope and y-intercept and use it to help you draw a graph of the line.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y = 5x-2</m>
          </p>
          <image width="45%">
      <shortdescription>blank xy-plane</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-xy-plane13" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>y = -\frac{2}{3}+4</m>
          </p>
          <image width="45%">
      <shortdescription>blank xy-plane</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-xy-plane14" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-6,-6,6,6)">
        <grid-axes arrows="2" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"
        hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-5,1,5)" hlabels="(-5,1,5)" vlabels="(-5,1,5)"/>
        </coordinates>
        </diagram>
        </prefigure>
        </image>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          For the followin equations, find the slope and y-intercept and use it to help you draw a graph of the line.
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>3x + 4y = 12</m>
          </p>
          <image width="45%">
      <shortdescription>blank xy-plane</shortdescription>
        <prefigure label="pf-xy-plane15" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
